\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces ADM masses of the spherical stars against the field oscillation frequency $\omega $, from Eqs.~\eqref  {eq:starode} at the working couplings: canonical stars ($M_{\rm  ADM}>0$, circles) and phantom stars ($M_{\rm  ADM}<0$, squares). Lower frequency means deeper binding and larger $|M_{\rm  ADM}|$; in the shaded region the solver finds no gravitationally dressed star. At equal frequency the phantom star is the heavier in magnitude ($20\%$ at $\omega =0.55$, $4\%$ at $\omega =0.80$), its repulsive self-gravity spreading it over more volume. Stars ($\star $): the equal-$|M_{\rm  ADM}|$ pair of run \texttt  {PM-eq}, matched to one part in $10^5$.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:family}{{2}{4}{ADM masses of the spherical stars against the field oscillation frequency $\omega $, from Eqs.~\eqref {eq:starode} at the working couplings: canonical stars ($M_{\rm ADM}>0$, circles) and phantom stars ($M_{\rm ADM}<0$, squares). Lower frequency means deeper binding and larger $|M_{\rm ADM}|$; in the shaded region the solver finds no gravitationally dressed star. At equal frequency the phantom star is the heavier in magnitude ($20\%$ at $\omega =0.55$, $4\%$ at $\omega =0.80$), its repulsive self-gravity spreading it over more volume. Stars ($\star $): the equal-$|M_{\rm ADM}|$ pair of run \texttt {PM-eq}, matched to one part in $10^5$}{figure.2}{}}
